A||||5||Viking||Finding balance between cornering/traction and handling||||6||
Z||000000||Viking||12-16-1999||10:05 PM||||I plan to change the rims and tires on my fiero in order to lower the tire profile.  I am also interested in filling in the empty space in the wheel well.  I am not willing to change the circumference on the tires, so I either need to lower the car OR fill it in with some type of panel.  A friend of mine thought that lowering the tire profile AND suspension would make it a horror to drive due to roughness of ride and awkwardness of height.  I'm open to any suggestions <BR>              Thanx||||reg||6
Z||000001||batboy||12-17-1999||12:07 AM||||I used lowering springs on the front of my Fiero to drop it about 2 inches and I am pleased with how it handles. But, the best way to lower the front with absolutely no sacrifice to handling is to use dropped control arms. Check out this link (go about halfway down the page).<P> <A HREF="http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.shelbycustom.com/addons/addons.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||12-17-1999||06:48 PM||||I disagree, I say that the only way to lower a vehicle without detriment to suspension is with dropped spindles.  With the dropped CA's you still screw up some of your geometry.  There are a couple of sources for dropped spindles specifically made for Fiero's.  Ryane Motorsports supposedly make them for all years.  There are some others (two I believe), but I don't remember them off of the top of my head.  If necessary, I could probably get that information for you.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-17-1999||08:13 PM||||What! Somebody disagrees with little ol' me? Well, maybe I should of said with little if any sacrifice to handling. Actually, I think you may have trouble finding those dropped spindles nowadays, besides, if you look at those dropped control arms, you'll see they do basically the same thing without changing the suspension geometry.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||12-17-1999||09:32 PM||||     I emailed Shelby Custom nearly a year ago about obtaining their dropped A-arms and they said they no longer make them.  They still have the add on their web page, which makes me think they are probably a fly-by night operation. Buyer beware.<P>Mike||||reg||13
Z||000005||batboy||12-17-1999||11:40 PM||||Thanks for the info. I've never ordered anything from them before, was just using that as an example. Like I said, I used lowering springs and poly suspension bushings and I've been happy. Coil-overs are probably even better and you have a much greater selection of spring heights and spring rates.||||reg||1
